From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 09:34:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] rtscom: Take a window's mouse input as a stylus, and build
 strokes from it.

PowerPoint's slide show pen drew nothing and a click with it moved to
the next slide.  Office Art's ink tool creates a RealTimeStylus, adds a
sync plugin, then makes a StrokeBuilder and adds it as an async plugin;
AddStylusSyncPlugin failed (the stylus was a stub) and StrokeBuilder was
not registered, so the tool threw and PowerPoint went without ink.

The RealTimeStylus now keeps its sync and async plugins, each with the
data interest it gives when added, its window and input rectangle, the
desired packet description (X, Y and normal pressure) and the tablet
mode.  Enabled, it takes the mouse input of its window through a mouse
hook on the window's thread, one hook for all styluses there: a button
press is StylusDown, moves while pressed are Packets, the release is
StylusUp, and moves over the window are in-air packets between
StylusInRange and StylusOutOfRange.  Packets are X and Y in HIMETRIC
from the client origin (and the packet status when asked for), for the
mouse as tablet context 1 and stylus 1.  Sync plugins are called on the
window's thread as the input comes, each seeing packets the ones before
it changed; async plugins are called afterwards from that thread's
queue.  GetPacketDescriptionData gives the mouse's metrics and the
scale from ink to device.  IRealTimeStylus2 and 3 keep their flick and
multitouch settings, and IRealTimeStylusSynchronization's locks are the
stylus's own lock.

StrokeBuilder makes strokes in its ink, a new one unless given one:
CreateStroke from all packets at once, or BeginStroke with the first,
AppendPackets and EndStroke, which gives the stroke's bounds as the
dirty rectangle.  As a sync or async plugin it builds a stroke of every
press of a stylus.  PowerPoint's pen now draws while dragged and keeps
the stroke, and View.DrawLine draws too.

Not measured on Windows yet: the tablet context and stylus numbers, the
default desired description, whether the mouse is a tablet by default,
the errors for enabling without a window or changing it while enabled,
and what EndStroke's dirty rectangle is in.  Tablets and cursors as
objects, child styluses, pen and touch input and pressure are not done.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

